\section{Stata's generate default}{

\code{\link[=gen]{gen()}} and \code{data[i, y := value]} on a column that does not exist are
translations of Stata's \code{generate}, and a bare double result takes its
default: \code{float}, or \code{double} under
\code{options(dtatools.generate_type = "double")}, the equivalent of Stata's
\verb{set type double}. A translated Stata line then keeps the storage the
original produced without restating it, and \code{\link[=dta_double]{dta_double()}} is the
explicit \verb{generate double}. A result that already carries storage keeps
it: a \verb{dta_*()} value, or arithmetic on a typed column, which declares
the storage of the Stata lattice described under \code{\link[=dta_byte]{dta_byte()}}, so
\code{gen(data, y = x * 2)} on a \code{double} column is \code{double}. The default
reaches a bare double: a literal, \code{NA_real_}, or the result of a base
function that drops the class. Bare integer results stay \code{long}, since
an R integer comes from R rather than from a Stata line and \code{float}
cannot hold one above 2^24. Every other entry point, including
\code{\link[dplyr:mutate]{dplyr::mutate()}} and the replacement operators, is an R operation on
the container and uses the mapping above, so the same expression can
take \code{float} through \code{gen()} and \code{double} through \code{mutate()}.

Columns no Stata storage can hold, such as raw, list, \code{difftime}, or
\code{bit64::integer64} columns, pass through a dibble unchanged and are
refused by \code{\link[=save_dta]{save_dta()}}. \code{\link[=gen]{gen()}} is stricter and rejects such a result.

A compact string from \code{\link[=read_arrow]{read_arrow()}} is declared with the width of its
dictionary and stays compact.
}

\section{Changing a typed column}{

When \code{\link[dplyr:mutate]{dplyr::mutate()}}, \verb{:=}, \code{transform()}, \code{within()}, or a
replacement operator overwrites a column that has declared storage, the
column keeps that storage if every new value fits it. Otherwise it takes
the narrowest Stata storage that holds every new value exactly: \code{byte},
\code{int}, \code{long}, \code{float}, then \code{double} for numbers; the smallest fitting
\code{str#} or \code{strL} for strings, and never a storage that narrows the
integers the column can hold, so an overflowing \code{long} goes to \code{double}
rather than through \code{float}, which carries seven fewer bits of integer
precision. This is Stata's \code{replace} ladder, which widens when the
declared type cannot represent a value at all, through range or through
integrality; \code{conformance/stata/replace-promotion.do} records it. The
one difference is precision, which never promotes in Stata: a \code{float}
given a value needing binary64 keeps \code{float} there and rounds, and goes
to \code{double} here, keeping the value exact. A value
that already carries storage, from a \verb{dta_*()} call or Stata-typed
arithmetic, keeps that storage. On a dibble the replacement operators
write by reference, so the promoted column is what every binding
holds. Row or cell assignment, as in
\code{data[1, "x"] <- 1000L}, promotes the same way, and a \verb{:=} whose value
declares wider storage than the column widens the column to it, as
\code{data[1, x := dta_double(1)]} makes \code{x} a \code{double}.
\code{\link[=replace_values]{replace_values()}} and \code{repl()} promote the
same way, and translate the message Stata's \code{replace} prints:
\code{variable `x` was byte now int}. Pass \code{promote = FALSE} to hold the
column to its declared storage and get an error instead. An assignment
that selects no rows promotes nothing, as Stata's
\verb{(0 real changes made)} does not. Promotion does not reach
\verb{[<-} on a Stata vector taken out of the dibble, as in
\code{data$x[1] <- 1000L}, which is the vector's own strict assignment.
}

\section{Bare tibbles and data frames}{

Only a dibble carries this contract. A tibble or data frame that has
been through \code{\link[=gen]{gen()}} carries reference state, but it is still a tibble
or a data frame: its existing columns stay unchanged, and only the columns
\code{\link[=gen]{gen()}} itself writes take Stata storage. \code{\link[=as_dibble]{as_dibble()}} is what types
the whole dataset. \code{\link[=save_dta]{save_dta()}} types bare columns by the same mapping
when it writes them, with logical as \code{byte}.

Plain Arrow files written by other tools, and files read with
\code{profile = FALSE}, carry no Stata semantics and read as tibbles by
default; ask for \code{output = "dibble"} to type them on the way in.
}

